**Presentations**, A structured delivery of information using visual aids to communicate ideas clearly and persuasively.

## Key points
- **Audience analysis**: identify the knowledge level, interests, and expectations of listeners to tailor content and tone.  
- **Structure**: intro (purpose, agenda), body (main points with evidence), conclusion (summary, call-to-action).  
- **Visual design**: use consistent fonts, high-contrast colors, minimal text, and relevant graphics; follow the 10-20-30 rule (max 10 slides, 20 minutes, 30-point font).  
- **Delivery skills**: maintain eye contact, modulate voice, use gestures, and rehearse transitions to avoid filler words.  
- **Technical tools**: proficiency with PowerPoint, Keynote, or LaTeX Beamer; know how to embed multimedia, hyperlinks, and animations safely.  
- **Feedback loop**: incorporate Q&A, solicit audience input, and adjust pacing based on non-verbal cues.

## Example, Beamer slide deck (LaTeX)

```latex
\documentclass{beamer}
\usetheme{Madrid}
\title{Design Patterns in Software Engineering}
\author{A. Student}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\frame{\titlepage}
\begin{frame}{Agenda}
  \tableofcontents
\end{frame}
\section{Singleton}
\begin{frame}{Singleton Pattern}
  \begin{itemize}
    \item Ensures a class has only one instance
    \item Provides a global point of access
  \end{itemize}
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{singleton.png}
  \end{center}
\end{frame}
\section{Observer}
\begin{frame}{Observer Pattern}
  \begin{block}{Definition}
    A one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one changes state, all dependents are notified.
  \end{block}
  \begin{center}
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\textwidth]{observer.png}
  \end{center}
\end{frame}
\section{Conclusion}
\begin{frame}{Key Takeaways}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item Patterns solve recurring design problems.
    \item Choose patterns based on context and constraints.
    \item Practice refactoring to apply patterns effectively.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{frame}
\end{document}
```

## Exam focus
1. **Question**: Explain the purpose of the agenda slide in a technical presentation.  
   **Answer**: It informs the audience of the structure and sets expectations for the flow of the talk.  

2. **Question**: What is the 10-20-30 rule and why is it recommended?  
   **Answer**: It limits slides to 10, time to 20 minutes, and font to 30 points to keep content concise and readable.
